#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084526 :tutorial/content "Let's plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny= \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\n. According to the algebraic definition of absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n{x\\begin{vmatrix}forx \\ge 0 \\\\ -x\\end{vmatrix}forx < 0\n$$\n\nSo, the left-hand branch of the graph is the function $y=-x$, and the right-hand branch is $y=x$:\n\n![](images/60/60ad4dfdb6086f20f5137c5b4ac4905e55d5697ec4cf31575bf79be5fd408e0c.png)\n\nNotice that the graph has a sharp corner at $(0,0)$. We call this point the **vertex** or the **minimum point.**\n\nWe can apply **horizontal** and **vertical translations** to the graph of\n\n$$\ny= \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\n.\n\n- The graph of $y= \\mid x \\mid +a$ is a **vertical translation** of the absolute value graph $y= \\mid x \\mid$. It moves the graph $a$ units up.![](images/bc/bc56f0d75b8359f71a104580902a18a1d8009091b94694beb7f8ac71c9bfc9bc.png)\n- The graph of $y= \\mid x-a \\mid$ is a **horizontal translation** of the absolute value graph $y= \\mid x \\mid$. It moves the graph $a$ units to the *right*.![](images/1f/1f79063f7350e734811366545507fc44c321882d2d9b228b85475a27d46c1759.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When drawing horizontal and vertical translations, it is easiest to transform the graph's vertex first. Then, the two branches can be drawn coming out of the vertex.\n- When we translate the graph $y= \\mid x \\mid$, the resulting curve is called the **image** of $y = \\mid x \\mid$ under the translation." :tutorial/id #uuid "1b8dce6a-8742-581b-80f6-e39c9943b395" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9295 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084528 :tutorial/content "Horizontal and vertical translations can be combined. It does not matter which we do first.\n\nIn general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny= \\mid x-h \\mid +k\n$$\n\nis a translation of the graph\n\n$$\ny= \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nby\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n- $k$ units up." :tutorial/id #uuid "b55739df-8b0d-5ef2-9702-d56de0246597" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9302 :tutorial/title "Combining Translations"}]}
